        <!-- SECTION 1: What N-P-K Actually Means for a Lawn -->
        <div id="npk-explained" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What N-P-K Actually Means for a Lawn
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every fertilizer bag carries three numbers, and for lawns specifically, only one of them usually matters much. Understanding why saves both money and unnecessary product.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Nitrogen, the first number,</strong> drives leaf growth and green color. Grass is grown entirely for its leaves, so nitrogen is the nutrient that produces almost everything a homeowner notices about a lawn. It is also the nutrient most readily lost from soil through leaching and volatilization, which is why lawns need it repeatedly while other nutrients often last for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Phosphorus, the second number,</strong> supports root development and is genuinely important for new seedlings. Established lawns, however, are rarely phosphorus-deficient, and phosphorus runoff into waterways causes algae blooms. That is why many states now restrict or ban phosphorus in fertilizer applied to established lawns, and why so many bags read 32-0-4 with a zero in the middle. Use phosphorus when seeding, and generally skip it otherwise unless a soil test says otherwise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Potassium, the third number,</strong> supports stress tolerance: drought resistance, cold hardiness, and disease resistance. It does not produce visible color, so it gets little attention, but it is why fall fertilizers often carry a higher potassium share to prepare turf for winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical implication is that for an established lawn, you are mostly buying nitrogen. A 32-0-4 and a 16-4-8 applied at rates that deliver the same nitrogen will produce broadly similar visible results, which means comparing bags by price per pound of actual nitrogen delivered is far more useful than comparing the sticker price of the bag.
          </p>

