        <!-- SECTION 2: Granular vs Liquid: Which You Actually Need -->
        <div id="granular-vs-liquid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Granular vs Liquid: Which You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            The format choice changes how fast you see results, how long the feeding lasts, and how much skill the application demands. Neither is universally better, and most good lawn programs use both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Granular Fertilizer</h3>

          <p>
            Granules dissolve gradually with watering and release nutrients into the soil over weeks. A quality slow-release granular feeds for six to eight weeks from a single pass, which makes it the efficient backbone of any fertilizer program. The cost is application skill: an uncalibrated spreader, an inconsistent walking pace, or poor overlap produces visible stripes that persist for weeks. Granular is also the only practical format for large lawns, since covering an acre with a hose-end sprayer is impractical.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Liquid Fertilizer</h3>

          <p>
            Liquids are absorbed partly through the leaf, so color change appears within two to three days rather than a week or more. Application through a hose-end sprayer is close to foolproof and produces genuinely even coverage without calibration. The trade-off is duration: two to three weeks per application means many more applications across a season and a higher total cost if liquid is your only fertilizer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Combination That Works Best</h3>

          <p>
            Use a slow-release granular as your baseline, applied roughly every six to eight weeks through the growing season, and keep a liquid on hand for a fast supplemental green-up when you want a visible improvement in days rather than weeks. This gives you the economics and duration of granular with the speed and evenness of liquid where it matters.
          </p>

