        <!-- SECTION 4: Quick-Release vs Slow-Release Nitrogen -->
        <div id="quick-vs-slow-release" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Quick-Release vs Slow-Release Nitrogen
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the specification that determines burn risk, how long a feeding lasts, and whether your lawn grows steadily or in surges. It is printed on every bag in the guaranteed analysis, and almost nobody reads it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Quick-release nitrogen</strong> is water-soluble and immediately available to the plant. It produces fast green-up, typically within three to five days, and it is cheap. It is also what burns lawns, because a heavy application delivers a salt load to the root zone faster than the plant can use it. Quick-release nitrogen is largely used up within two to four weeks, after which growth slows sharply until the next application.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Slow-release nitrogen</strong> is either coated so it dissolves gradually, or bound in an organic form that soil microbes must break down. Either way it becomes available over six to ten weeks, producing steady growth instead of a surge, dramatically lower burn risk, and less nitrogen lost to leaching. It costs more per unit of nitrogen and takes longer to show results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most good consumer fertilizers blend the two, using quick-release for visible early results and slow-release for duration. On the bag, look for the percentage listed as slowly available or water-insoluble nitrogen. A product where a substantial share of total nitrogen is slow-release will feed longer and burn less than one that is nearly all quick-release, and that difference is far more meaningful than a few points of difference in the headline analysis number.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical consequence is that comparing two bags by their first number alone is misleading. A 32-0-4 that is almost entirely quick-release and a 24-0-6 that is largely slow-release will behave completely differently in your lawn, and the second will generally produce a better result over a season despite the smaller number on the front.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Building a Feeding Schedule by Season -->
        <div id="feeding-schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Building a Feeding Schedule by Season
          </h2>

          <p>
            When you fertilize matters as much as what you use. The schedule below is written for cool-season lawns, with warm-season adjustments noted, and it assumes roughly four applications a year, which suits most homeowners.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Early Spring</h3>

          <p>
            A light feeding as the lawn breaks dormancy, ideally combined with a crabgrass pre-emergent if you are not seeding. Resist the urge to apply heavily here. Cool-season grass is already growing vigorously in spring, and pushing it harder produces excessive top growth at the expense of root development, plus a lot of extra mowing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Late Spring</h3>

          <p>
            A second feeding roughly six to eight weeks after the first, timed before summer heat arrives. This is the last heavy nitrogen application cool-season lawns should receive until fall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Summer</h3>

          <p>
            Cool-season grasses slow down or go dormant in heat, and pushing nitrogen into a heat-stressed lawn invites disease and burn. If you feed at all in summer, use a low-rate organic product like Milorganite, which carries no burn risk. Warm-season lawns are the opposite case: summer is their peak growth period and their heaviest feeding window.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Early Fall</h3>

          <p>
            The most valuable feeding of the year for cool-season grass. Soil is still warm, air is cooling, and nitrogen goes into root growth and carbohydrate storage rather than blade growth. This is also the ideal time to overseed. If you only fertilize once a year, do it now.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Late Fall</h3>

          <p>
            A final feeding roughly four to six weeks before the ground freezes, sometimes called a winterizer, builds reserves that drive early, strong spring green-up. Warm-season lawns should not receive a late-season nitrogen application, since tender new growth going into frost is easily damaged.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Application: Spreaders, Rates, and Avoiding Burn -->
        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Application: Spreaders, Rates, and Avoiding Burn
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good fertilizer applied badly produces a worse lawn than a mediocre fertilizer applied well. These are the details that separate the two outcomes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Calibrate the spreader, do not trust the bag setting.</strong> Spreader settings printed on fertilizer bags are approximations across many spreader models and wear conditions. The reliable method is to measure a known area, weigh out the correct amount of product for it, and adjust until you use that amount over that area. A worn or dirty spreader throws differently than a new one at the same setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a broadcast spreader and half rates in two passes.</strong> Applying at half the rate in two perpendicular passes rather than full rate in one pass dramatically reduces striping, because the gaps from one direction get covered by the other. It takes marginally longer and produces a visibly more uniform result.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water in after applying.</strong> Granular fertilizer needs water to dissolve and move into the root zone. Watering in also washes granules off leaf blades, which is where burn happens, since a granule sitting on a wet blade concentrates salt against plant tissue. A quarter inch of irrigation after application is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Never fertilize a drought-stressed or dormant lawn.</strong> Grass under water stress cannot use nitrogen and is far more vulnerable to salt injury. If the lawn is browning from heat and lack of water, water it and let it recover before feeding.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">How Lawn Burn Actually Happens</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Fertilizer burn is osmotic, not chemical. A concentrated band of soluble salts in the soil pulls water out of grass roots rather than letting water in, and the plant dehydrates. That is why burn appears as sharply defined stripes or spills matching exactly where product was over-applied, why it is far worse in heat and drought, and why watering in immediately after application prevents most of it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Soil Testing and Why It Changes the Answer -->
        <div id="soil-testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Soil Testing and Why It Changes the Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every recommendation in this guide is a reasonable default in the absence of information. A soil test replaces defaults with facts, costs very little through most county extension services, and occasionally reveals that the reason a lawn looks bad has nothing to do with fertilizer at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>pH is the most common hidden problem.</strong> Grass takes up nutrients most efficiently in a soil pH range of roughly 6.0 to 7.0. Outside that range, nutrients present in the soil become chemically unavailable to the plant, which means you can fertilize a lawn repeatedly and see minimal response because the nitrogen is there but the grass cannot access it. Acidic soil is corrected with lime, alkaline soil with elemental sulfur, and both take months to shift, which is why testing early matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Phosphorus and potassium levels rarely need supplementing.</strong> Most established lawns test adequate or high in both, which is exactly why so many fertilizers now carry a zero for phosphorus. Applying nutrients the soil already has in sufficient quantity wastes money and, in the case of phosphorus, contributes to water pollution with no benefit to your lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Organic matter tells you about water retention.</strong> A soil low in organic matter drains fast, holds little water, and needs more frequent irrigation and feeding. Raising it is slow work through topdressing with compost, returning clippings rather than bagging them, and using organic fertilizers, but it changes the underlying performance of the lawn rather than treating symptoms.
          </p>

          <p>
            A test through a county extension office typically costs less than a single bag of fertilizer and is valid for around three years. For anyone who has fertilized diligently and been disappointed with the result, it is the most useful next step available.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best fertilizer for grass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most lawns, Scotts Turf Builder Lawn Food offers the best balance of visible results, six to eight week duration, and forgiving application. Milorganite is the safest choice with essentially no burn risk, and The Andersons PGF Complete 16-4-8 produced the most uniform, professional-looking result in our testing thanks to its small dispersing granules.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should I fertilize my lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For cool-season lawns, early fall is the single most valuable feeding of the year because nitrogen goes into root growth and carbohydrate storage rather than blade growth. A typical program is a light early spring feeding, a late spring feeding, an early fall feeding, and a late fall winterizer. Warm-season lawns feed most heavily through summer instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do the numbers on fertilizer mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are the percentages of nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium by weight. Nitrogen drives leaf growth and green color, phosphorus supports root development and matters most for new seed, and potassium supports drought, cold, and disease tolerance. For established lawns, nitrogen is the number that matters most, which is why many bags read 32-0-4.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I avoid burning my lawn with fertilizer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a product with a high slow-release nitrogen fraction, calibrate your spreader rather than trusting the bag setting, apply half rates in two perpendicular passes, water in with about a quarter inch of irrigation immediately after applying, and never fertilize a lawn that is drought-stressed or dormant. Organic products like Milorganite carry essentially no burn risk at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is liquid or granular fertilizer better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Granular is the better backbone because a slow-release application feeds for six to eight weeks and is more economical across a season. Liquid is absorbed through the leaf and shows color change in two to three days, and a hose-end applicator makes even coverage nearly automatic, but it only lasts two to three weeks. Most good programs use granular as the base and liquid as a supplement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I fertilize and seed at the same time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but use a starter fertilizer containing phosphorus rather than a standard high-nitrogen lawn food, since phosphorus supports the root development new seedlings need. Never use a combination weed and feed product containing a crabgrass pre-emergent when seeding, because that pre-emergent prevents grass seed from germinating just as effectively as it prevents crabgrass.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Lawn Fertilizing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fertilizer problems are rarely about which bag you bought. They are almost always about rate, timing, or the state of the lawn when you applied it.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying a Weed and Feed in a Season You Plan to Seed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The crabgrass pre-emergent in a combination product blocks lawn seed from germinating exactly as effectively as it blocks crabgrass. Every year homeowners apply both within a few weeks of each other and conclude they bought bad seed. Choose one or the other for a given spring, and seed in fall where possible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Heavy Spring Feeding on a Cool-Season Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cool-season grass is already in a vigorous growth phase in spring, and heavy nitrogen then produces excess top growth at the expense of root development, plus considerably more mowing. It also leaves the lawn shallow-rooted heading into summer heat. Feed lightly in spring and save the heavy application for fall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Spreader Setting Printed on the Bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Those settings are approximations across many spreader models, and a worn or dirty spreader throws very differently from a new one. The result is either wasted product and burn risk from over-application, or disappointing results and visible stripes from under-application. Calibrate over a measured area once and the number is good for years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fertilizing a Drought-Stressed or Dormant Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grass under water stress cannot take up nitrogen, and the concentrated salts pull moisture out of already stressed roots. Applying fertilizer to a browning summer lawn does not revive it, it damages it further. Water first, let the lawn recover, then feed once it is actively growing again.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

